  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Japanese min'yō (\"folk song of the people\") encompasses the regional-traditional song repertoire of Japan, with distinct regional canons across Tohoku (northern Honshu, distinctive for vigorous work-song and sake-brewing-song lineages), Kyushu (with the Itsuki Lullaby and broader Kyushu-folk repertoire), Okinawa (separate from mainland Japanese min'yō with sanshin-and-ryuka-poetic-meter tradition), and Hokkaido (Soran Bushi the canonical Hokkaido min'yō, originally an Okhotsk Sea herring-fishing work-song). Performance configuration: solo voice with shamisen, shakuhachi flute, and taiko drum accompaniment. The post-1920s commercial-recording period at Nippon Columbia and Polydor Japan documented canonical regional repertoires.",
